What is the historical context of the Post-9/11 GI Bill, including its purpose and significance?
The Post-9/11 GI Bill was signed into law in July 2008, creating a new education benefits program that provides comprehensive education benefits to veterans who served on active duty for 90 or more days since September 10, 2001. The bill was designed to provide veterans with the skills and training they need to be successful in the future, as part of President Obama's plan to support veterans. The bill was created in response to the 9/11 terrorist attacks, which led to significant changes in U.S. politics and foreign policy. The Post-9/11 GI Bill was intended to rival the WWII Era GI Bill of Rights and provide benefits to current and previously activated National Guard and Reserve members, as well as active-duty service members.
