What is a single-parent family, and how does it differ from other family types?
A single-parent family is a household consisting of a single adult (most often a woman) and one or more children, where the adult cares for the children without the assistance of the other biological parent. This can be due to various reasons such as divorce, break-up, death of one parent, abandonment by one parent, single-parent adoption, or artificial insemination.

Single-parent families differ from other family types in that they have only one adult responsible for child-rearing, whereas other family types may have two biological parents or stepparents. Single-parent families can be further categorized into sole parent (where one parent manages all responsibilities without financial or emotional assistance) and co-parent (where both parents share responsibilities despite not living together).
