What is the structure and organization of the Jewish calendar, including its months and their corresponding numbers?
The Jewish calendar is a lunisolar calendar, meaning that its months are based on the lunar cycle, but its years are based on the solar year. The calendar features 12 lunar months, which are:

1. Nisan (usually corresponding to March-April)
2. Iyar (usually corresponding to April-May)
3. Sivan (usually corresponding to May-June)
4. Tammuz (usually corresponding to June-July)
5. Av (usually corresponding to July-August)
6. Elul (usually corresponding to August-September)
7. Tishrei (usually corresponding to September-October)
8. Marheshvan (usually corresponding to October-November)
9. Kislev (usually corresponding to November-December)
10. Tevet (usually corresponding to December-January)
11. Shevat (usually corresponding to January-February)
12. Adar (usually corresponding to February-March)

The months can have either 29 or 30 days, with an extra month (Adar I or Adar II) added every 2-3 years to keep the calendar in sync with the solar year. The extra month is added to the month of Shevat or Adar, depending on the year.
