Explain the context and significance of the introduction of the 'in-group' and 'out-group' concept in social psychology.
The introduction of the 'in-group' and 'out-group' concept in social psychology was made popular by Henri Tajfel and colleagues in their work on social identity theory. This concept refers to the way people categorize themselves and others into social groups, with an 'in-group' being a social group to which a person psychologically identifies as a member, and an 'out-group' being a social group with which an individual does not identify.

The significance of this concept lies in its association with various phenomena, including in-group favoritism, where people tend to favor members of their own group over out-group members. This concept has been linked to group conflict and prejudice, and has been researched extensively in social psychology. The introduction of this concept has provided a framework for understanding how people form and maintain social groups, and how these groups influence behavior and attitudes towards others.
