Describe the process of carbon fixation in C4 plants, including the role of the C4 pathway and the key enzymes involved.
In C4 plants, carbon fixation occurs through the C4 pathway, which involves the fixation of CO2 into a four-carbon molecule, malate, in the mesophyll cells. The process begins with the conversion of pyruvate to phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) by pyruvate orthophosphate dikinase, followed by the fixation of CO2 into oxaloacetate by PEP carboxylase. The malate is then transported to the bundle sheath cells, where it is converted back into CO2 and PEP by the enzyme NADP-malic enzyme (malate dehydrogenase). The CO2 is then fixed into 3-phosphoglycerate by the enzyme RuBisCO, bypassing the photorespiration pathway. The key enzymes involved in the C4 pathway are pyruvate orthophosphate dikinase, PEP carboxylase, NADP-malic enzyme, and RuBisCO.
